WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.288 --> 00:00:07.811
Johannes Gutenberg's workshop was a beehive of activity.

2
00:00:08.692 --> 00:00:15.854
His new printing press worked perfectly, and thousands of printed pages rested in neat, orderly piles.

3
00:00:16.815 --> 00:00:22.997
Gutenberg hoped that when all the pages of the Bible were completed and the copies were sold, he

4
00:00:23.037 --> 00:00:28.360
would make enough money to pay his debts and settle comfortably into a printing business for the rest

5
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:28.940
of his life.

6
00:00:29.868 --> 00:00:31.049
But it was not to be.

7
00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:40.156
Building the printing press, casting the type (thousands of individual letters and characters), and developing the perfect ink

8
00:00:40.576 --> 00:00:42.838
had taken much longer than he expected.

9
00:00:43.958 --> 00:00:51.664
Gutenberg's partner and financial backer, a wealthy businessman named Johannes Fust, became impatient.

10
00:00:52.665 --> 00:00:56.988
He wanted the money he had lent Gutenberg and he wanted it immediately!

11
00:00:58.068 --> 00:01:07.393
The actual printing was turning into a monumental task—each Bible contained 322 large sheets of paper that would

12
00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:11.315
be folded into 1,286 pages.

13
00:01:12.296 --> 00:01:19.180
Gutenberg had invested all of Fust's money into the project, but had not received any money yet from

14
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.580
orders or sales.

15
00:01:21.521 --> 00:01:28.489
When Gutenberg couldn't pay, Fust took him to court, and the judge gave the press, the equipment, and

16
00:01:28.709 --> 00:01:30.930
even Gutenberg's workshop to Fust.

17
00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:38.373
Very little is known about Johannes Gutenberg, and what little is known has been recovered from the public

18
00:01:38.433 --> 00:01:41.374
court records of the many lawsuits filed against him.

19
00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:50.978
He was born sometime around 1400, in Mainz, Germany, a time when, and a place where, life centered

20
00:01:51.138 --> 00:01:58.233
on the Holy Roman Catholic Church and the most important religious leader in Germany: the Archbishop of Mainz.

21
00:01:59.534 --> 00:02:08.481
For generations, Gutenberg's family held a hereditary position in the archbishop's mint, the church office responsible for managing

22
00:02:08.521 --> 00:02:10.843
the money flowing in and out of the church.

23
00:02:11.944 --> 00:02:19.509
Gutenberg's father and grandfather were money changers, people who replaced old coins with new ones they had made

24
00:02:19.949 --> 00:02:23.072
and who exchanged foreign coins for local money.

25
00:02:23.964 --> 00:02:29.768
In order to fulfill their duties, the Gutenberg family men would have been very skilled working with all

26
00:02:29.828 --> 00:02:32.210
types of metals, including gold.

27
00:02:33.251 --> 00:02:42.057
About 1434, after his mother died and his family estate was dissolved, Gutenberg moved to Strasbourg and became

28
00:02:42.117 --> 00:02:46.039
a member of the goldsmiths' guild, earning a living as a jeweler.

29
00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:55.002
Europeans at the time were fascinated by lenses, mirrors, and prisms, and Gutenberg wanted to capitalize on a

30
00:02:55.142 --> 00:03:01.428
huge pilgrimage to the cathedral in Aachen, Germany, being planned for 1439.

31
00:03:02.368 --> 00:03:08.754
He decided to make small badges with convex mirrors on them to sell to the thousands of pilgrims

32
00:03:08.854 --> 00:03:09.474
at the event.

33
00:03:10.695 --> 00:03:19.483
Religious people believed that holy relics—pieces of clothing or bones from a dead saint or important religious figure—had

34
00:03:19.543 --> 00:03:23.630
the power to heal, and that this power could be captured by mirrors.

35
00:03:24.811 --> 00:03:30.196
Gutenberg thought the badges would sell very well during the part of the festival when the relics would

36
00:03:30.236 --> 00:03:31.698
be displayed to the worshippers.

37
00:03:32.718 --> 00:03:39.585
Unfortunately, the pilgrimage was cancelled due to the plague, and Gutenberg did not make the money he expected

38
00:03:39.665 --> 00:03:40.425
from the venture.

39
00:03:41.286 --> 00:03:45.830
But soon Gutenberg was pursuing another idea: a printing press.

40
00:03:46.951 --> 00:03:53.908
At this time, books were copied by hand by monks in monasteries and by scribes in workshops close

41
00:03:53.948 --> 00:03:54.829
to universities.

42
00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:02.154
The process took years and books were owned only by churchmen, academics, and aristocrats.

43
00:04:03.095 --> 00:04:09.540
They were copied on parchment made from sheep or goat skin and on vellum made from calf skin.

44
00:04:10.861 --> 00:04:19.298
Around the year 1442, Gutenberg returned to Mainz and began concentrating on the creation of a new printing

45
00:04:19.358 --> 00:04:27.061
process. From the press to the type to the ink, Gutenberg invented each working part of his printing

46
00:04:27.141 --> 00:04:34.724
system or else modified existing devices in order to print copies of excellent quality in a fraction of

47
00:04:34.744 --> 00:04:35.224
the time.

48
00:04:36.484 --> 00:04:46.481
As a printer in that era, Gutenberg was already using moveable wooden type—individually carved wooden letters—to compose words,

49
00:04:46.581 --> 00:04:48.843
sentences, paragraphs, and pages.

50
00:04:49.924 --> 00:04:57.411
But these wooden letters were time-consuming to make and tended not to wear well over time, which resulted

51
00:04:57.611 --> 00:05:00.273
in an inferior quality of printed copy.

52
00:05:01.194 --> 00:05:10.242
Being a goldsmith, Gutenberg knew how to use steel dies—metal tools for stamping designs and letters—and metal punches

53
00:05:10.322 --> 00:05:12.524
for imprinting seals and coins.

54
00:05:13.644 --> 00:05:19.025
Gutenberg put these two concepts together and developed moveable metal type.

55
00:05:20.066 --> 00:05:27.188
He also created an alloy, a mixture of tin, lead, and antimony, which was soft enough to be

56
00:05:27.348 --> 00:05:31.729
easily made into type, but hard enough to last for a long time.

57
00:05:32.709 --> 00:05:36.370
The quality of printing achieved with metal type was excellent.

58
00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:45.578
Gutenberg even developed a handheld mold to cast the letters quickly and easily, a great innovation since one

59
00:05:45.658 --> 00:05:48.881
page required about three thousand characters.

60
00:05:50.042 --> 00:05:56.907
To hold the letters, spaces, and punctuation marks in place to compose a page, he devised a form,

61
00:05:57.428 --> 00:06:04.314
and since the water-based inks of the fifteenth century did not adhere to the metal type, Gutenberg developed

62
00:06:04.474 --> 00:06:08.137
an oil-based ink rich in lead and copper.

63
00:06:09.358 --> 00:06:15.549
And finally, he devised a press, based on the wine and paper presses of the day, to put

64
00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:20.250
even pressure on each page as it came into contact with the inked letters.

65
00:06:21.651 --> 00:06:30.113
Many scholars believe that around 1452, Gutenberg began to print indulgences for the church—documents that were bought by

66
00:06:30.153 --> 00:06:33.014
the faithful to obtain forgiveness of sins.

67
00:06:33.974 --> 00:06:39.696
Gutenberg printed and sold thousands of these documents to the church to make enough money to print the

68
00:06:39.736 --> 00:06:50.026
Bible. With his own money, and financing from his partner Johannes Fust, Gutenberg began production in 1455 on

69
00:06:50.066 --> 00:06:53.749
the Bible, his most important and enduring project.

70
00:06:54.949 --> 00:07:00.913
About 150 Gutenberg Bibles were printed and 48 still survive today.

71
00:07:01.954 --> 00:07:06.837
Some were made of vellum and others of paper, which was now being produced in Europe.

72
00:07:07.878 --> 00:07:14.706
They were printed on both sides of the page, each page consisting of 2 columns of 42 lines

73
00:07:14.906 --> 00:07:22.089
each. The margins were even and straight, the words stretched or compressed to avoid hyphens.

74
00:07:23.349 --> 00:07:31.693
Gutenberg's type carefully replicated the style of letters used by the scribe monks, and as in books produced

75
00:07:31.753 --> 00:07:38.396
by scribes, color was added to some capital letters by hand as well as to decorations in the

76
00:07:38.436 --> 00:07:46.689
margins. Many believe that the quality of Gutenberg's Bible is unsurpassed, even when compared to Bibles that were

77
00:07:46.769 --> 00:07:48.270
printed many years later.

78
00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:56.936
According to scientists, it is Gutenberg's ink that allows us to easily read his surviving Bibles; more than

79
00:07:57.196 --> 00:08:02.940
550 years later, his print is still sharp, glossy, and black.

80
00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:11.925
Unfortunately, the production process took longer than expected and Gutenberg, running out of money and unable to sell

81
00:08:12.126 --> 00:08:17.649
any Bibles until all the pages were completed and bound together, could not pay his debts to his

82
00:08:17.709 --> 00:08:26.133
partner. But Johannes Fust demanded his money before the project was finished, and some scholars believe he deliberately

83
00:08:26.233 --> 00:08:29.936
cheated Gutenberg so he could reap the benefits of Gutenberg's work.

84
00:08:31.216 --> 00:08:38.481
All that is known is that the suit Fust brought against Gutenberg to recover his loan stripped Gutenberg

85
00:08:38.601 --> 00:08:41.403
of everything he had developed for printing the Bible.

86
00:08:42.264 --> 00:08:50.530
By 1456, Fust, along with one of Gutenberg's assistants, had completed the printing of the Bible; he then

87
00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:53.312
sold the printed copies and profited from them.

88
00:08:54.213 --> 00:08:56.875
Gutenberg received absolutely nothing.

89
00:08:57.876 --> 00:09:08.532
In 1465, Adolph II, archbishop of Mainz, belatedly recognized Gutenberg's achievement, gave him a pension, and brought the

90
00:09:08.712 --> 00:09:14.494
aging printer into his court, where Gutenberg remained until his death in 1468.

91
00:09:15.614 --> 00:09:22.796
Gutenberg's printing press accelerated the progress and complexity of society in that it led to the production of

92
00:09:22.896 --> 00:09:25.497
books and newspapers on a grand scale.

93
00:09:26.457 --> 00:09:34.039
This increase in publishing promoted literacy, education, and the exchange of scientific and political ideas.

94
00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:44.894
With mass communication, innovation flourished; so although he never profited from his genius, Johannes Gutenberg changed the world.
